use  sometimes I get to score films in two  weeks and it's just crazy and but you  can't you have to I mean you can but  it's here you really I had to really dig  in  and especially because Nina has never  worked with a composer before it was a  new experience for her I kind of walked  her through the process  so I told Nina just let me do the first  scene and then we'll talk and after I  score the first scene which is about  four minutes or so she called me she  said yes yes yes that's exactly what I  wanted and from that it was a much  um easier process  but  first she's very meticulous which I like  you know because I find that when I  score a film the director's notes  usually make my music better so I'm very  open to that but emotionally how do you  bring about all these  very dark emotions as a woman  without being over dramatic and that was  I think  the challenging part and there were a  few points when I was doing a bit more  and she's like no no don't give me Star  Wars what I want is this subtle  um  um nuances of the film and you know  after I did the first 20 minutes or so I  told after the first 20 minutes  everything is established and everything  will be a smooth sailing from that and  which is exactly what happened but  um like the scene  on the plane when she is like hand-tied  and I was like  it was really hard for me to see it  because you're like she's so hopeless  I'm helpless I'm sorry not hopeless  maybe hopeless as well but it's like you  know I think we need to talk about these  issues and we need to address them  and I know that not everybody is going  to like this film because you know kind  of puts a mirror on  Hollywood and I'm part of Hollywood and  you're part of Hollywood and I love  Hollywood but these are things that we  need to talk about I was wondering if  as accomplished artists and filmmakers  yourselves  if  examining this film really taking in its  meaning  if that's changed your art  has that changed the way that you feel  